### Self-Compassion as Daily Purpose Practice

Kristin Neff's self-compassion research has three components: **self-kindness** (treating yourself with warmth rather than judgment), **common humanity** (suffering is part of shared human experience, not a personal failure), and **mindfulness** (holding pain in balanced awareness without over-identifying). Self-compassion is often confused with self-esteem — but self-esteem requires you to be good enough to deserve it, while self-compassion is unconditional.

**The worthiness trap:** People who grew up in high-pressure or conditional environments often treat purpose as a way to finally be good enough. This turns purpose into another performance metric — exhausting, never-satisfied, never-arriving. Self-compassion separates inherent worth from conditional achievement.

**The daily practice — 30 seconds:** When you fail or feel inadequate: (1) "This is a moment of suffering." (2) "Suffering is part of the human experience." (3) "May I be kind to myself in this moment." Self-compassion does not reduce motivation — shame does. People high in self-compassion take more responsibility and persist longer.

**The self-compassion break:** When a difficult moment arises, take 30 seconds to place both hands on your chest and say: *"This is hard right now. I'm not alone in this. May I be kind to myself."* This interrupts the shame cascade.

> The person who can extend compassion to themselves during failure is the person who will keep showing up.

→ Full content: [Self-Compassion Practice](references/self-compassion-practice.md)

## When to Recommend Professional Support

Suggest therapy, a career coach, or a support group when:
- Feelings of purposelessness are accompanied by persistent sadness, hopelessness, or self-harm thoughts
- The user is actively dissociating from daily life (not showering, not leaving house, not eating)
- There is a history of multiple career/relationship changes that all feel "wrong" — this can indicate a deeper pattern
- The user explicitly says "I think I might be depressed"

**Scripts for acute distress:**

**"I'm in a dark place right now" / "I don't want to be here anymore":**
> I'm not the right person for this conversation right now. What you need is human connection and professional support — someone who can be with you in real time. Please reach out to a crisis line (text or call 988 in the US) or go to your nearest emergency room. We can talk about purpose work when you're safe. I'll still be here.

**"Everything feels hopeless" / "there's no point to anything":**
> Hopelessness can be a symptom of depression — and depression is treatable. This is not about finding your purpose right now; it's about getting support so you can get back to a place where purpose becomes visible again. Talking to a therapist is not a sign of weakness — it's one of the clearest signals you're willing to fight for yourself.

**"I think I might be depressed":**
> Thank you for saying that directly. That takes honesty and courage. I'd like to suggest talking to a professional — a therapist can help you understand what's happening and give you tools that make a real difference. Would you like help finding one, or do you already have someone you're working with?

> The purpose frameworks in this skill are not designed for clinical depression. They work best when the person has enough stability to engage with them. The first and most important step is getting support — everything else can follow.

